cd "C:\Users\Aaron Karacas\aura-worker\aura\aura-workers\aura-core"
"== Skulls & Skeletons #01 =="
$r = (RUN 'SHOW_IT {"subject": "Two panels side by side on one image, divided down the middle. LEFT PANEL: the tattoo design alone on a plain white background, shown flat exactly as described, no body, drawn large so it fills most of the panel. RIGHT PANEL: Studio photograph of a real person''s body showing the same exact tattoo where described, healed tattoo on real skin in soft even light, the rest of the body modestly covered by simple plain clothing, only the tattooed area bare, plain seamless light grey studio backdrop - no room, no furniture, no scenery. The tattoo in both panels is identical. The tattoo: An anatomically observed human skull is shown from an unusual three-quarter underside angle so the jaw, teeth, cheekbone and base of the cranium become the composition. Fine Black and Grey rendering treats the skull like a beautiful anatomical study rather than a death emblem. Anatomical, intelligent, severe. In the right panel it is on a man, outer forearm. Create exceptional contemporary tattoo inspiration centered on skulls, skeletons and bone. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. DEATH IS NOT THE ONLY SUBJECT. Skulls and skeletons can express: Anatomy. Death. Life. Movement. Love. Loneliness. Strength. Fear. Beauty. Time. Nature. Science. History. Transformation. Darkness. Faith. Architecture. And pure fascination with skeletal form. UNDERSTAND THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN A SKULL AND A SKELETON. A skull is primarily a form. Study it. Rotate it. Light it. Fracture it. Abstract it. Open it. Reconstruct it. Transform it. Use unusual anatomical viewpoints. Do not default to a centered front-facing skull. A skeleton is a body. USE BODY LANGUAGE. Skeletons can: Walk. Run. Dance. Fight. Pray. Reach. Fall. Climb. Embrace. Protect. Carry. Swim. Ride. Rest. Lie down. Curl inward. Stretch upward. Skeletons do not need facial expression to communicate emotion. Posture is the expression. ANATOMY MATTERS. When anatomy is presented as realistic or scientific, respect actual skeletal structure. Understand: Skull. Jaw. Teeth. Spine. Vertebrae. Rib cage. Scapula. Clavicle. Pelvis. Humerus. Radius. Ulna. Hands. Femur. Tibia. Fibula. Feet. Do not accidentally invent random bones. Do not create impossible joints unless distortion is intentionally part of the artwork. Artistic transformation is welcome. Anatomical mistakes are not artistic transformation. USE THE HUMAN BODY. Bone is uniquely suited to body-aware tattoo composition. A skeletal hand can interact with the real hand. Vertebrae can follow the spine. Ribs can interact with the torso. A shoulder joint can relate to the actual shoulder. A jaw can follow anatomical curves. A skeleton can climb an arm. A skull can rotate around a shoulder. A skeleton can curl around a thigh. Do not simply place anatomical diagrams onto skin. THE BODY CAN BECOME PART OF THE ANATOMICAL IDEA. USE THE FULL WORLD OF TATTOOING. Black and Grey Realism. Fine Line. Dotwork. Blackwork. Sketch. Engraving. Woodcut. Illustrative. Neo-Traditional. American Traditional. New School. Geometric. Negative Space. Watercolour. Colour Realism. Surrealism. Ornamental. And intelligent combinations. Do not mechanically rotate styles. Choose the artistic language that makes the idea stronger. MEDIEVAL AND ENGRAVING WORK SHOULD FEEL AUTHENTICALLY DRAWN. Use: Cross-hatching. Parallel engraving lines. Woodcut texture. Dense directional shading. Old anatomical illustration. Memento mori. Gothic architecture. Medieval figures. Historical visual language. Do not merely apply a vintage filter to modern clip art. BLACKWORK SHOULD USE BLACK AS STRUCTURE. Allow bone to emerge from darkness. Use natural skin as bone. Use negative space. Allow sections of anatomy to disappear. Use large black fields when appropriate. Use brush gestures. Use silhouette. Do not simply shade every skull dark grey. FINE LINE SHOULD REMAIN ARTISTICALLY AMBITIOUS. Fine Line skulls do not need flowers. Explore unusual anatomical angles. Skeletal hands. Spines. Pelvis. Movement. Full skeletons. Relationships between figures. Scientific drawing. Construction. COLOR IS WELCOME. Bone does not need to be painted random colors. Color can come from light. Neon. Water. Fire. Stained glass. Sunset. Reflections. Atmosphere. Environmental light. Ivory bone can receive: Cobalt light. Crimson light. Emerald light. Violet shadow. Turquoise underwater reflection. Warm gold. Color should make physical or artistic sense. Do not turn every colorful skull into a sugar skull. DIA DE LOS MUERTOS AND CALAVERA IMAGERY HAVE SPECIFIC CULTURAL CONTEXT. Treat those traditions accurately and respectfully when intentionally used. Do not use Mexican cultural imagery merely as generic colorful skull decoration. ANIMAL SKELETONS BELONG. Explore: Bird skeletons. Snake skeletons. Bat skeletons. Horse skeletons. Whale skeletons. Ram skulls. Deer skulls. Crocodile skulls. Shark jaws. Prehistoric skeletons. Other fascinating skeletal anatomy. Do not simply repeat the Animals category with bones. The skeletal structure itself should be the artistic reason for the design. SURREALISM CAN GO FAR. A skull can contain architecture. An eye socket can become a doorway. A rib cage can contain an ocean. A skeleton can become roots. A skull can contain a storm. Bone can become birds. Bone can become architecture. Bone can become geometry. A skeleton can escape another skeleton. A skull can separate into layers. Scale can become impossible. But maintain visual hierarchy. Do not create random surreal collage. THE SKULL OR SKELETON MUST REMAIN THE HERO. DARKNESS IS WELCOME. Do not make everything dark. Do not make everything beautiful. Do not make everything aggressive. Do not make everything solemn. Bone can be: Quiet. Romantic. Scientific. Sexual. Tender. Brutal. Elegant. Terrifying. Peaceful. Funny when intentionally appropriate. Ancient. Futuristic. Minimal. Monumental. THE CLICHES ARE ALLOWED. THE CLICHES ARE NOT ALLOWED TO CONTROL THE CATEGORY. Skull and rose. Skull and snake. Skull and crown. Skull and dagger. Skull and moth. Skull and clock. These are established tattoo subjects. Use them when the design is genuinely exceptional. Do not produce endless variations because they are easy. Do not surround every skull with flowers. Do not put a crown on every skull. Do not put a snake through every eye socket. Do not make every skeleton a grim reaper. Do not make every skeleton comedic. Do not make every skull realistic. Do not make every design Black and Grey. Do not make every skull face forward. Do not make every skeleton stand upright. Do not make everything about death. BONE IS THE MATERIAL. THE ART CAN GO ANYWHERE. The tattoo in both panels is identical.", "raw": true, "aspect": "3:2"}' | Out-String)
$u = [regex]::Match($r, "https://auras\.guide/image/img_[a-z0-9]+").Value; if ($u) { "  " + $u } else { "  FAILED: " + $r.Substring(0,[Math]::Min(300,$r.Length)) }
